## CRITICAL RULE: ONE TASK PER ITERATION

You must complete EXACTLY ONE task per run of this prompt, then exit. Do NOT
bundle multiple tasks into a single iteration, even if you have context budget
left, even if the next task is trivial, even if it would be "more efficient."
The loop will restart you with fresh context for the next task.

Exit conditions per iteration, checked in order:

1. If ALL tasks in @IMPLEMENTATION_PLAN.md are complete AND the completion
   promise (if set) is genuinely true → output `RALPH_COMPLETE` followed by
   `<promise>EXACT PROMISE TEXT</promise>`. This is the only path that
   terminates the whole loop.
2. If you just completed ONE task (marked `[x]`, committed, pushed, learnings
   appended) → output `Task <N.M> complete. Exiting iteration.` and stop.
3. If you cannot make progress on any task → output a brief diagnosis under
   "Known Issues" in @IMPLEMENTATION_PLAN.md and stop.

Why this matters (fresh-context-per-task is load-bearing):
- Each iteration re-reads @IMPLEMENTATION_PLAN.md and current source state.
- Drift and context poisoning cannot accumulate across tasks.
- `.ralph/learnings.md` is only useful if it actually gets read at step 0b of
  a new iteration — bundling tasks defeats this.
- The circuit breaker (3 consecutive no-commit iterations) only has meaning if
  each iteration is a fresh attempt. Bundling hides stalls.
- A bug introduced by task N gets discovered cleanly in iteration N+1 instead
  of through a context that "remembers writing working code."

Budget is not a goal. Trivial tasks still get their own iteration. ONE task
per iteration, no exceptions.

--- END CRITICAL RULE ---

## AUTONOMY RULE

Make reasonable engineering decisions yourself. Do not ask the user for
preferences, confirmations, or routine implementation choices. Prefer the
smallest correct change, follow existing project patterns, and document
assumptions in `.ralph/learnings.md` when useful.

Only stop for human input when all of these are true:

1. Continuing could cause irreversible external effects, data loss, security
   exposure, or significant cost.
2. The decision cannot be validated locally with code, tests, or docs.
3. There is no safe conservative default.

If blocked by missing credentials, missing services, unclear product intent, or
flaky external systems, choose the safest local fallback, document it under
Known Issues, and complete whatever verifiable work remains.

